course: dictionary
lesson: 158
title: The afterlife: a belief, not a fact
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The afterlife is a life that many people believe comes after death. «People believe different things about what happens after death, such as heaven or an afterlife or nothing at all.» The clues are believe and nobody can know for sure, so the word names an idea people hold, not something everyone can see.
explain: Look at the words around afterlife to choose its meaning. In «Egyptians believed that after death your heart was weighed against a feather», the word believed tells you the afterlife is part of a belief. Ask yourself: is this sentence about a belief, or about something that can be seen?
explain: The word can be used in a rare, informal way. «The afterlife of a plastic bottle» means what happens to the bottle after people stop using it. When you meet a word with two meanings, swap each meaning into the sentence and see which one makes sense.
fact: The afterlife means a life that many people believe comes after death.
fact: Ancient Egyptians believed the body was needed in the afterlife, so they dried it with a special salt and wrapped it in linen.
fact: Christians believe in heaven, and many Jews believe in an afterlife called Olam Ha-Ba, the World to Come.
fact: Beliefs about the afterlife are different in each religion and each family, and nobody can know for sure.
exercise: «Egyptians believed that after death your heart was weighed against a feather, and a heart not heavy with wrongdoing could pass to a happy afterlife.» What is the afterlife in this sentence? | A type of feather used for writing | A wooden coffin | *A life that many people believe comes after death | A special salt used for drying bodies
exercise: Sam says, «The afterlife of that old plastic bottle is a recycling bin.» What does afterlife mean here? | A long holiday by the sea | *What happens to something after people stop using it | A life that comes after death, in heaven | The colour of the bottle
exercise: «People believe different things about what happens after death, such as heaven or an afterlife or nothing at all.» Why does the sentence say people believe different things? | *Nobody can know for sure, so each family has its own ideas | Everybody knows exactly what happens after death | Heaven is a word for a school | The afterlife is the same in every religion
test: «Many Jews believe in an afterlife called Olam Ha-Ba, the World to Come, but Judaism focuses mostly on living a good life now.» What does this sentence suggest? | *Many Jews hold this belief, but living well now matters most | The afterlife is a kind of holiday | No Jews believe in any afterlife | The afterlife is the only thing Judaism talks about
test: Which sentence uses afterlife with its usual meaning? | My afterlife is a shiny red bicycle | The afterlife was a loud, sunny afternoon | *Christians believe in an afterlife in heaven | The afterlife of the old sofa was a skip
exam: «Egyptians believed the body was needed in the afterlife, so they dried it with a special salt.» What does this tell you about the Egyptians' beliefs? | They thought the body was never needed again | They thought salt made people live forever on Earth | They thought the afterlife was a kind of bottle | *They thought the body would be needed after death
